## msil-ld's checks of managed calls (ImplementationPlan.md 5.20.7 MK2-MK4
## and MK7, milestone MC-1; texts T-M7, T-M8, T-M9, T-M16, T-M18 and
## T-M23 of 5.20.3, pinned whole; '<f>' is the C name, without the
## '__msil_mc.' prefix). Most inputs have one managed call; the references
## are Inputs/fakepack/FakeWidgets.il over FakeRuntime.il (see
## managed-calls.test).
## - T-M7 (MK2): an integer passed for a 'valuetype E' parameter needs an
##   enum E whose value__ has that integer's width:
##   'managed call '<f>': parameter <i> passes <n> bits for '<E>', which is not an enum of that width',
##   <i> being the member's 1-based CIL parameter position: an int32 for an
##   int64-backed enum (wide.ll) and for a uint8-backed enum (small.ll), a
##   struct passed as an integer (struct.ll: Contoso.Point), the third CIL
##   parameter (third.ll), and the first CIL parameter after a 'this' handle
##   (tune.ll), on a host triple and on msil64-unknown-dotnet. The widths
##   that match (an int64 for WideMode, a signed char for SmallMode, an int
##   for Mode) link: managed-calls.test.
## - T-M7 for each parameter on its own (MK2; MC-1's fix of D1): a
##   'boxed' handle of a value type V exempts only its own parameter,
##   never another parameter of V that C passes as an integer. pairs.ll
##   calls Pair(Point, Point) and SetPair(Mode, Mode) with a handle beside a
##   long long, in both orders: T-M7 for the integer's parameter (2, or 1),
##   64 bits for the struct Contoso.Point and for the int32-backed enum
##   Contoso.Mode, on x86_64-pc-windows-msvc-msil,
##   x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu-msil and msil64-unknown-dotnet. These links
##   used to succeed silently, the integer reaching the value-type parameter
##   unchecked (a stub that PEVerify refuses). Two handles, and a handle
##   beside an int for the enum, link (pairs-ok.ll).
## - T-M7 in its result form (MK2 as amended in section 12, 2026-11-04): a
##   'valuetype E' result (for a field member, the field's type) that the C
##   result takes as an integer ('enum') needs an enum E whose value__ has
##   the width of the stub's return type:
##   'managed call '<f>': the result has <n> bits for '<E>', which is not an enum of that width':
##   a struct result taken as an int (origin-int.ll: Contoso.Point), an
##   int64-backed enum result read into an int (wide-res.ll, on a host
##   triple and on msil64-unknown-dotnet) and an int64-backed enum field
##   read into an int (wide-field.ll). The widths that match link
##   (res-ok.ll: an int32-backed enum result into an int; field-ok.ll: the
##   int64-backed field into a long long).
## - The same check for C 'char' and 'short' results (the row 'fix-loop
##   ownership: ... MSILMCTypeUtilities.h -> fix-6' of section 12,
##   2026-11-04: the stub's MethodDef has the exact return type int8 or
##   int16, so the object check and the --lto-emit-asm check of
##   managed-calls-lto.test read the same width): an int32-backed enum
##   result read into a short (res16.ll, Contoso.Mode, as a
##   Console::get_ForegroundColor() read into a short would be) is T-M7's
##   result form with 16 bits, on a host triple and on msil64-unknown-dotnet;
##   a uint8-backed enum result read into a signed char (res8-ok.ll,
##   Contoso.SmallMode) and an int16-backed one read into a short
##   (res16-ok.ll, Contoso.ShortMode) link, and their stubs return int8 and
##   int16.
## - MK2's stub shape (5.20.7 MK2 as amended in section 12, 2026-11-04): the
##   access of a 'this-val' method is 'call V::M', 'constrained. V' then
##   'callvirt V::M' (llc's placeholder, which msil-ld rewrites), or
##   'constrained. V' then 'callvirt D::M' with D the bound member's
##   declaring type. patch-access.py turns the placeholder of shape.ll's stub
##   (point_sum: Contoso.Point::Sum()) into 'constrained. GCHandle' then
##   'callvirt Contoso.Point::Sum()', in none of the three shapes, which
##   MK2 reports as an internal error (an object that needs a matching
##   toolchain) instead of linking or rewriting it:
##   '<file>: managed call '<f>': the stub calls '<X>::<M>' through 'constrained. <V>', but '<V>' has no such member; the object needs a matching toolchain'.
## - T-M8 (MK2, MK3): without references nothing tells an enum from a
##   struct, so an enum conversion or a value type other than GCHandle is
##   'managed call '<f>': '<V>' is a value type, which msil-ld checks only against references; pass --reference=<reference assemblies>':
##   an enum parameter (mode.ll; its member is outside the table, so the
##   binder's '.NET member 'Contoso.Widget' is not in msil-ld's built-in
##   mscorlib table' line follows it, the next line, and no other error), a
##   value-type TYPE (point.ll, likewise, naming 'Contoso.Point'), and a
##   value-type TYPE whose member the table binds (exc-val.ll:
##   'valuetype System.Exception' with get_HResult(), only T-M8), static
##   members included (MC-1's fix of MK3: vt-table.ll,
##   'valuetype System.Threading.Interlocked' with the table's static
##   MemoryBarrier(), only T-M8; it used to link). GCHandle is exempt:
##   managed-calls.test's table.ll links GCHandle::Free() without
##   references, and gch-static.ll links the static GCHandle::Alloc(object)
##   on a 'valuetype' TYPE without references.
## - T-M9 (MK4): with references, an msil_catch type must derive from
##   System.Exception:
##   'managed call '<f>': 'msil_catch' type '<T>' does not derive from System.Exception'
##   (notexc.ll: Contoso.NotAnException). Types that derive through other
##   types (Contoso.BrokenWidgetException through WidgetException,
##   DecoderFallbackException through ArgumentException and
##   SystemException) link: managed-calls.test.
## - T-M16 (MK3): with references, the 'valuetype' prefix and the boxed and
##   'this' conversions must agree with the TypeDef:
##   'managed call '<f>': '<T>' is a <value|reference> type, but the declaration uses it as a <reference|value> type'
##   (refval.ll: 'valuetype Contoso.Widget' with a 'this-val' get_Size();
##   valref.ll: 'Contoso.Point' with a 'this-obj' Sum()), and for the
##   conversions of parameters and constructors: a 'boxed' parameter of a
##   class spelled 'valuetype' (boxedref.ll: unbox.any Contoso.Widget), an
##   'obj' parameter of a struct spelled 'class' (objval.ll: castclass
##   Contoso.Point), a struct's constructor whose TYPE is spelled as a class
##   (ctorval.ll: Contoso.Point, 'val,val;obj', newobj without box, on a
##   host triple and on msil64-unknown-dotnet) and a class's constructor
##   whose TYPE is spelled as a value type (ctorref.ll: 'valuetype
##   Contoso.Widget', 'utf8,val;boxed', newobj then box). A parameter's
##   spelling is part of the member's signature, so for boxedref.ll and
##   objval.ll the binder's undefined-member error follows. A class spelled
##   'valuetype' as the TYPE of a static method or a static field is T-M16
##   too (MK3, checked for every member kind; these used to link):
##   vt-static.ll ('valuetype Contoso.Widget' with Guarded(int32)),
##   vt-field.ll (with the static field Count), on a host triple and on
##   msil64-unknown-dotnet, and vt-table.ll (the FakeRuntime class
##   System.Threading.Interlocked with MemoryBarrier()).
## - MK3's parents (MC-1's fix of MK3): a static member of a
##   'valuetype' TYPE is a MemberRef whose parent is a TypeSpec in the object
##   ('valuetype [msil.bcl]Contoso.Point::Twice'), which msil-ld writes in
##   the image as the TypeRef parent that IL and C# use
##   ('[FakeWidgets]Contoso.Point::Twice'): pt-static.ll, the static method
##   Twice(int32) and the static field Total of the struct Contoso.Point, on
##   a host triple and on msil64-unknown-dotnet. GCHandle keeps a TypeRef
##   parent in the object too (gch-static.ll). A value type named without
##   'valuetype' as the TYPE of a static member keeps its TypeRef parent and
##   links (pt-plain.ll), as in IL: the prefix matters where the kind
##   changes the code (an instance member's 'this', a constructor's result).
## - T-M18 (MK7, section 12, 2026-11-04): with references, a field member
##   whose FieldDef is a literal has no storage:
##   'managed call '<f>': reading the literal field '<T>::<F>' is not supported for MSIL yet'
##   (literal.ll: 'field int32' Contoso.Widget::Max, on a host triple and
##   on msil64-unknown-dotnet).
## - T-M23 (MC-1's fix of D11): a managed-call stub
##   '__msil_mc.<symbol>' is a name that only the toolchain defines, as an
##   internal function (a LOCAL FUNCTION symbol of the object), so an input
##   that defines it otherwise is refused:
##   '<file>: defines '__msil_mc.<symbol>', which is reserved for the MSIL toolchain (a managed-call stub)':
##   t23b.ll defines a GLOBAL '__msil_mc.widget_count' (a C function with
##   that asm label) beside t23a.ll's managed call widget_count, which linked
##   silently, and t23b2.ll's definition calls ext1() and ext2() (t23c2.ll),
##   which msil-ld used to check as the object's stub ('the stub's body
##   makes more than one member access'), on x86_64-pc-windows-msvc-msil
##   and msil64-unknown-dotnet. A hand-written internal stub (t23ok.ll, an
##   "msil-managed-stub" definition as slot P0 makes one) is a LOCAL symbol
##   and links. managed-calls-lto.test has the bitcode cases, and
##   managed-calls-forwarders.test the forwarder form, for definitions of
##   '__msil_mc_ctor.<s>' and '__msil_mc_dtor.<s>'.
## msil-ld prints each text right after 'msil-ld: error: ': T-M7, T-M8,
## T-M9, T-M16 and T-M18 with no file prefix, T-M23 and the stub-shape
## error after the object's name as the RUN line gives it.
## A failed link writes no output. The same checks under LTO are
## managed-calls-lto.test's.

# REQUIRES: llvm-cil-dump
# RUN: rm -rf %t && split-file %s %t && cd %t
# RUN: mkdir refs
# RUN: yaml2obj %S/Inputs/fakepack/FakeRuntime.yaml -o refs/FakeRuntime.dll
# RUN: yaml2obj %S/Inputs/fakepack/FakeWidgets.yaml -o refs/FakeWidgets.dll
# DEFINE: %{crash} = --implicit-check-not="PLEASE submit" --implicit-check-not="Stack dump" \
# DEFINE:   --implicit-check-not="LLVM ERROR"
# DEFINE: %{err} = FileCheck %s %{crash} --implicit-check-not=error: --implicit-check-not=warning:

## T-M7.
